:"w","headline":"17.Rxc6!! A real sacrifice: White gives up material and the engine still rates it best.","points":["Puts the rook on c6 where it can be taken.","Black can take, but after 17…Bxc6 18.Rxe7 Bb5 19.Qa1 the engine rates it +0.79.","Takes the knight on c6.","17.Rxc6 wins a knight for a rook.","Takes the knight on c6 and wins a knight for a rook once the captures settle.","Black answers 17…Bxc6, taking back."],"why":[{"title":"The idea","text":"17.Rxc6!! offers the rook on c6. Taking it gives White the material back and more."},{"title":"What White gives up","text":"17.Rxc6 puts the rook on c6 where it can be taken by the queen on d5, the bishop on b7 and the rook on c8. That's a whole rook on offer."},{"title":"What it does instead","text":"It takes the knight on c6."},{"title":"If Black takes the rook","text":"17…Bxc6 is met by 18.Rxe7, taking the bishop on e7. Then 19.Rxa7 takes the a7 pawn, and 21.Bd4 attacks the b6 pawn, which is undefended. White ends up a pawn ahead: White is a little better (+1.22). This is Black's best try."},{"title":"Without the sacrifice","text":"The best move without the sacrifice, 17.Qd2, is clearly weaker (0.00 against +0.79). Black answers 17…Qd6. That would leave White level."}]},{"ply":39,"name":"20.Qa1+","cls":"great","color":"w","headline":"20.Qa1+! The only good move here: every alternative is clearly worse.","points":["The queen joins the attack: it now hits 2 squares around the Black king.","Protects the a2 pawn, which was hanging.","The next best move, 20.Ne1, would drop White's winning chances from 53% to 14% (−4.86).","20.Qa1+ joins the attack on the king and protects the a2 pawn.","Gives check, so the reply is forced.","Black answers 20…Kg8, winning a bishop."]},{"ply":41,"name":"21.Bxb5","cls":"blunder","color":"w","headline":"21.Bxb5 is a blunder: White's winning chances go from 54% to 32%.","points":["Black answers 21…Qxb5, which takes the bishop on b5.","After Qxb5, Black wins 3 points of material within 2 moves.","Better was 21.Re5 (+0.49), which skewers the queen: when it moves, the bishop on b5 behind it falls."],"better":"21.Re5 threatens Rxd5, winning the queen, and stops …Bxd3. After 21…Qxd3 22.Rg5+ Qg6 23.h4 f6 24.Rxg6+ hxg6 25.Nd4, White comes out a pawn up (+0.49)."},{"ply":42,"name":"21…Rfd8","cls":"miss","color":"b","headline":"21…Rfd8 misses 21…Qxb5, which wins material. Black's winning chances go from 68% to 13%.","points":["White answers 22.Bf1, which moves the attacked bishop to safety.","Better was 21…Qxb5 (−2.00), which takes the bishop on b5."],"better":"21…Qxb5 wins a bishop for a pawn and stops Re5. After 22.h3 Rfe8 23.Rxa7 Qc6 24.Nh4 Qc1+ 25.Qxc1 Rxc1+ 26.Kh2, the queens come off and Black comes out two pawns up (−2.00)."}],"opening":{"name":"Queen's Pawn Game: Symmetrical Variation","eco":"D02","points":["Both sides followed opening theory up to 6…dxc4: 12 moves in all, 6 by White and 6 by Black.","Theory runs out after 6…dxc4: too few games between strong players reached this position to go further."]}}